1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
On this season of LAiDIES: she stops
feeling behind.

2
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:08,380
She learns to delegate to machines —
without lowering her standards.

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:11,640
She builds her own little squad of AI
helpers.

4
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:15,940
And she becomes the woman other people
come to when the future gets confusing.

5
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:20,560
And on this episode: our heroine finally
stops putting it off.

6
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:25,860
This is Episode One: On Wednesdays We
Do AI.

7
00:00:25,860 --> 00:00:29,780
It's 4:52 on a Tuesday afternoon, and a
man named Steve is being called a
visionary. Steve works in revenue
operations.

8
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:33,340
Steve has never once refilled the
printer.

9
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:39,650
And Steve has just presented a
competitive analysis — clean, confident,

10
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:45,261
suspiciously well-formatted — that
everyone in that room knows took him

11
00:00:45,261 --> 00:00:49,780
about an hour, because at lunch he was
playing pickleball.

12
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:55,120
I know because he told us. Twice. And
me?

13
00:00:55,880 --> 00:01:02,160
I had a version of that same analysis.
Mine took two weekends. Mine had
footnotes.

14
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:08,200
Mine was still sitting in my drafts
folder, waiting until it felt... ready.

15
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:14,480
Somewhere between Steve's standing
ovation and my fourth coffee, it hit me.

16
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:16,940
He isn't smarter than me.

17
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:18,920
He isn't better informed.

18
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:22,300
He just stopped doing it the hard way.

19
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:28,140
And I couldn't help but wonder... when
did everyone learn to do that?

20
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:32,620
And when, exactly, was I supposed to?

21
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:38,969
Welcome to LAiDIES — where smart, busy
women learn AI one Wednesday at a time,

22
00:01:38,969 --> 00:01:41,920
from a little internet town called
SUNNYVAiLE.

23
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:46,820
New here? check out the trailer first.
It's the tour.

24
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:49,720
This is where the story starts.

25
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:54,020
And that "she" from the promo? That's
me.

26
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:59,341
I'm telling this from a few steps ahead
— I've made the mistakes, done the

27
00:01:59,341 --> 00:02:03,921
reading, and kept the receipts — and as
the season goes on, you'll hear from

28
00:02:03,921 --> 00:02:09,780
other women figuring it out in real
time. This isn't a lecture. It's a group
chat.

29
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:16,113
So if Steve sounded familiar — if you
have ever sat on work that was better

30
00:02:16,113 --> 00:02:20,940
than what got the applause — get in
loser. We're learning AI...

31
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:31,220
So. Let me tell you what I did after the
Steve meeting. Nothing. For six months.

32
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:34,100
Well — not nothing.

33
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:39,225
I did what every competent woman does
with a problem she doesn't have time

34
00:02:39,225 --> 00:02:40,680
for: I put it on a list.

35
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:46,280
And in my defense, every time I tried to
start, the on-ramp was terrible.

36
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:53,922
Everything I found was either written by
men in fleece vests — say no more — or

37
00:02:53,922 --> 00:03:01,080
so surface-level it basically amounted
to "AI is transformative!" AI is
transformative?

38
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:07,000
Groundbreaking. Here's the thing. I have
a full-time job.

39
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:08,580
I manage a team.

40
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:12,620
My calendar is a Tetris game I am
perpetually losing.

41
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:18,014
So the idea of adding "become
AI-literate" to that pile felt about as

42
00:03:18,014 --> 00:03:23,880
realistic as Miranda Priestly asking me
to fetch the unpublished Harry Potter
manuscript.

43
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:28,700
Technically possible — but at what
personal cost?

44
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:35,194
Then one night — eleven p.m., everyone
else asleep, glass of wine — I went

45
00:03:35,194 --> 00:03:37,300
looking for a different answer.

46
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:42,180
And somewhere past the third search
result... I found a town.

47
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:47,974
An actual little internet town,
permanently set in nineteen ninety-nine,

48
00:03:47,974 --> 00:03:52,580
with a main street, a radio station, and
a library that never closes.

49
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:55,240
It's that kind of place.

50
00:03:56,140 --> 00:03:58,660
I didn't know it yet, but I was moving
in.

51
00:03:59,460 --> 00:04:05,907
And that first night, in that library,
the question I brought wasn't "how do I

52
00:04:05,907 --> 00:04:08,600
learn AI." It was: is it just me?

53
00:04:09,820 --> 00:04:11,640
It was not just me.

54
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:13,860
I found a Harvard study.

55
00:04:15,940 --> 00:04:17,480
A hundred and forty thousand people,
around the world.

56
00:04:18,220 --> 00:04:23,360
For every one hundred men using
generative AI tools... seventy-eight
women.

57
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:30,440
And that gap holds even when you control
for job type, age, education, and
access.

58
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:35,580
Same tools. Same desks. Different
adoption rates.

59
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:39,460
And the deeper I read, the worse it got.

60
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:45,420
Men are twenty-three percent more likely
to be encouraged by their managers to
use AI.

61
00:04:47,420 --> 00:04:49,800
Twenty-seven percent more likely to be
praised when they do.

62
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:54,480
Steve got called a visionary, remember.
And women?

63
00:04:55,480 --> 00:05:01,500
We're thirty-two percent more likely to
worry that using AI looks like cutting
corners.

64
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:07,640
That one hit me like Samantha Jones
delivering an uncomfortable truth over
brunch.

65
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:11,200
Because of course we worry about that.

66
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:16,318
We've spent entire careers building
credibility through preparation,

67
00:05:16,318 --> 00:05:18,200
thoroughness, visible effort.

68
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:24,766
And now there's a tool that makes hard
things look easy — and some part of our

69
00:05:24,766 --> 00:05:31,460
brain calculates, correctly, based on
historical data, that "easy" might get
held against us.

70
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:34,240
That's not imposter syndrome.

71
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:36,240
That's pattern recognition.

72
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:42,020
Which is ironic, given what AI actually
is — but we'll get there.

73
00:05:43,260 --> 00:05:47,799
You know how there's always one person
who preps the deck before the meeting,

74
00:05:47,799 --> 00:05:52,280
remembers the feedback from last time,
follows up on the action items nobody

75
00:05:52,280 --> 00:05:55,480
else tracked — and still delivers her
own work on time?

76
00:05:56,880 --> 00:05:59,760
That person is usually not named Steve.

77
00:06:00,660 --> 00:06:03,500
No offense to the Steves out there
fighting the good fight.

78
00:06:04,380 --> 00:06:09,900
So no — this was never a confidence
problem. It's a physics problem.

79
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:13,780
You cannot add hours to a day that's
already over-subscribed.

80
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:20,463
And the irony is, the tool that could
give you your time back requires time

81
00:06:20,463 --> 00:06:24,480
you don't have to learn it. So you don't
start.

82
00:06:25,560 --> 00:06:30,864
And the gap compounds, week over week,
until the distance between you and the

83
00:06:30,864 --> 00:06:36,480
colleague who started six months ago
isn't a gap anymore. It's a canyon.

84
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:44,320
As Dolly would say... You'd better get
to building your own bridge, honey —
because ain't nobody building it for
you.

85
00:06:45,020 --> 00:06:50,170
One more thing I found that night, from
Fei-Fei Li — the Stanford professor they

86
00:06:50,170 --> 00:06:53,700
call the Godmother of AI. She said it
plainly:

87
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:59,041
If we don't get women involved in AI,
we're going to have a future built by

88
00:06:59,041 --> 00:07:01,820
half the population — for all of the
population.

89
00:07:03,020 --> 00:07:05,120
And it's not just the principle of the
thing.

90
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:10,975
When women don't use these tools, the
tools learn from a skewed pool of users

91
00:07:10,975 --> 00:07:13,420
— they literally get worse for women.

92
00:07:14,580 --> 00:07:16,100
And around the cycle goes.

93
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:20,480
So this was never just about my career,
or yours.

94
00:07:21,640 --> 00:07:27,680
So here's what finally got me to start.
It wasn't inspiration. It was spite.

95
00:07:28,820 --> 00:07:34,723
Fine — it was mostly spite, plus a
Sunday morning at the Blend and Snap —

96
00:07:34,723 --> 00:07:40,707
my corner table, an oat latte going
cold, the radio on low — fifteen quiet

97
00:07:40,707 --> 00:07:43,020
minutes, and nothing to lose.

98
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:46,900
There was an email I'd been avoiding for
four days.

99
00:07:47,720 --> 00:07:53,880
You know the kind — the delicate one, to
the stakeholder who reads tone into line
breaks.

100
00:07:54,940 --> 00:08:00,165
I opened one of the AI tools, and
instead of asking it something grand, I

101
00:08:00,165 --> 00:08:05,532
just told it the truth: who the email
was for, what I needed from them, and

102
00:08:05,532 --> 00:08:07,580
what I couldn't say out loud.

103
00:08:08,300 --> 00:08:11,000
It gave me a draft in nine seconds.

104
00:08:12,060 --> 00:08:15,320
And it was... eighty percent right.

105
00:08:16,460 --> 00:08:21,764
The other twenty percent was wrong in
ways only I could see — which, it turns

106
00:08:21,764 --> 00:08:23,260
out, is the good news.

107
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:27,640
I fixed it with my own judgment. I hit
send.

108
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:34,340
And then I looked at the clock. Four
days of dread. Eleven minutes of work.

109
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:40,920
And that's when it landed: oh. I can do
this.

110
00:08:42,140 --> 00:08:45,100
Because the thing stopping me was never
ability.

111
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:49,860
It was that nobody had explained it in a
way that made me want to start.

112
00:08:50,680 --> 00:08:52,280
Now — I want to be clear.

113
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:55,120
I am no AI slayer.

114
00:08:56,020 --> 00:09:01,820
There's no Watcher guiding me through
some prophecy, no training montage that
happened off-screen.

115
00:09:01,820 --> 00:09:07,900
I'm still learning, still getting things
wrong, still googling things
mid-conversation.

116
00:09:08,920 --> 00:09:13,320
But here's my favourite stat I've found
so far, and it's the one that keeps me
going.

117
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:19,820
BCG looked at senior women in tech — the
ones who push through that first awkward
phase.

118
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:21,840
And they're not trailing the men.

119
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:27,081
They're out in front — already using
these tools more than their male peers,

120
00:09:27,081 --> 00:09:30,120
by fourteen percentage points. Fourteen.

121
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:36,184
Not because they had some head start —
but because they were more aware it

122
00:09:36,184 --> 00:09:38,380
mattered, and more willing to take the
leap.

123
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:42,820
You don't claw your way to senior
without learning to bet on yourself.

124
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:48,840
And what they bring to it is the one
thing AI cannot replicate: a career's

125
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:50,940
worth of professional judgment.

126
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:56,820
The instinct that something is off in a
document before you can even articulate
why.

127
00:09:57,700 --> 00:10:00,700
That twenty percent of my email only I
could see?

128
00:10:01,480 --> 00:10:02,980
That's your critical thinking.

129
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:06,760
Which brings me to my favourite part of
every episode.

130
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:11,648
We call it the cocktail party
explanation, and it was born at the

131
00:10:11,648 --> 00:10:17,000
Bronze Age — that's our bar in
SUNNYVAiLE — during businesswomen's
special happy hour.

132
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:22,427
I was three sips into telling my
girlfriends about the email thing when

133
00:10:22,427 --> 00:10:26,440
one of them put down her drink and said,
"okay, but what IS it?

134
00:10:26,980 --> 00:10:31,860
Actually?" The table went quiet.
Everybody wanted to know.

135
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:34,540
Nobody wanted to be the one to ask.

136
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:40,861
So now, every week, this segment hands
you the answer you'll give the next time

137
00:10:40,861 --> 00:10:45,962
somebody at a dinner table — or a happy
hour — says "AI" like they know what

138
00:10:45,962 --> 00:10:47,420
they're talking about.

139
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:53,560
This week's question is the big one:
what is this thing, actually?

140
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:56,760
And I'll give you exactly what I gave
the girls.

141
00:10:57,660 --> 00:11:02,380
It's the most talented new hire you'll
ever manage. That's it.

142
00:11:03,080 --> 00:11:08,036
It fits in the lull before the drinks
arrive, and it holds up under every

143
00:11:08,036 --> 00:11:10,380
follow-up — so let me show you why.

144
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:16,653
A brilliant new hire has read
everything: books, articles, forums,

145
00:11:16,653 --> 00:11:22,622
manuals, billions of documents — it's
absorbed how language works, how ideas

146
00:11:22,622 --> 00:11:25,180
connect, how arguments get built.

147
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:27,980
That's why it sounds so impressive.

148
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:34,488
And it has lived nothing: no job, no
relationships, no consequences, not one

149
00:11:34,488 --> 00:11:39,620
awkward moment at a holiday party that
taught it something about people.

150
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:45,120
So the honest picture isn't a robot
genius, and it isn't a toy.

151
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:52,226
It's day-one talent: superhuman range,
astonishing speed, first drafts that'll

152
00:11:52,226 --> 00:11:58,255
genuinely scare you — and no read on
your office politics, no stake in what

153
00:11:58,255 --> 00:12:02,260
happens if it's wrong. That's where you
come in.

154
00:12:03,080 --> 00:12:06,200
You don't hand it the keys — you onboard
it.

155
00:12:06,700 --> 00:12:10,760
You give it guidance and guardrails, the
lay of the land around the office.

156
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:14,240
You manage it, and you review its work.

157
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:17,020
And you've done this all before.

158
00:12:18,440 --> 00:12:23,883
Now — under the hood, when you type
something into ChatGPT, or Claude, or

159
00:12:23,883 --> 00:12:29,840
Gemini, what it's doing is prediction —
generating what comes next, word by
word.

160
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:34,700
And here's the strange part: it's still
just that, underneath.

161
00:12:35,580 --> 00:12:41,085
But what grew out of all that prediction
— it drafts, it reasons, it'll even run

162
00:12:41,085 --> 00:12:45,639
multi-step errands for you — is
impressive enough that the experts

163
00:12:45,639 --> 00:12:51,620
genuinely can't agree on what to call
it, or even whether "reasoning" is the
right word.

164
00:12:52,420 --> 00:12:54,340
We'll get to all of that later in the
season.

165
00:12:55,220 --> 00:13:00,567
But two limits are not up for debate
(and yes, in this case the limits DO

166
00:13:00,567 --> 00:13:06,420
exist) — and they're the two that decide
whether it helps you or embarrasses you.

167
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:12,000
Limit one: it does not know your context
until you hand it over.

168
00:13:13,720 --> 00:13:18,426
It's Cher's closet computer from
Clueless: endless outfit combinations,

169
00:13:18,426 --> 00:13:23,394
all day long — but it has no idea the
meeting is with a conservative client

170
00:13:23,394 --> 00:13:28,100
who is already looking for reasons to
dismiss you... unless you tell it.

171
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:35,160
Remember my email? I told it the truth
about who it was for, and it used every
word of that.

172
00:13:36,060 --> 00:13:37,720
The context lives with you.

173
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:43,480
Handing it over well — that's a skill,
and it's literally next week's episode.

174
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:50,280
Limit two: out of the box, it does not
check whether what it says is true.

175
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:52,440
Only whether it's plausible.

176
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:58,531
It's the Burn Book from Mean Girls: some
entries accurate, some completely

177
00:13:58,531 --> 00:14:02,740
fabricated, all delivered with the same
unbothered confidence.

178
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:10,520
"Made out with a hot dog"? Same
certainty as an actual fact — it has no
way of telling the two apart.

179
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:14,500
Regina George energy. But make it AI.

180
00:14:15,320 --> 00:14:20,154
Three words before you go, so you don't
pull a Cher, confidently arguing it

181
00:14:20,154 --> 00:14:23,080
doesn't say R.S.V.P. on the Statue of
Liberty.

182
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:30,220
Generative AI: the kind that makes new
things — text, images, first drafts.

183
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:36,386
A Carrie Bradshaw in your laptop — not
one that finds you articles to read,

184
00:14:36,386 --> 00:14:40,000
that's Google — one that writes the
column for you.

185
00:14:42,040 --> 00:14:43,800
Model: the trained brain behind the app.

186
00:14:45,940 --> 00:14:50,311
ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini are the
magazines; the models are their

187
00:14:50,311 --> 00:14:54,940
editors-in-chief — different taste,
different judgment, different style.

188
00:14:55,780 --> 00:14:58,500
Same brief, wildly different results.

189
00:14:59,660 --> 00:15:06,217
And hallucination: when the answer comes
back polished, confident... and partly

190
00:15:06,217 --> 00:15:08,020
or completely made up.

191
00:15:08,840 --> 00:15:12,460
It's not lying — lying takes intent.
It's guessing.

192
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:17,992
The newer models are better at it —
they'll flag when they're unsure, and if

193
00:15:17,992 --> 00:15:21,000
you hand them a source, they'll actually
check it.

194
00:15:21,720 --> 00:15:26,934
But better isn't fixed: push one past
what it really knows and it'll still

195
00:15:26,934 --> 00:15:28,880
answer with total authority.

196
00:15:30,020 --> 00:15:34,960
Think of your most confident friend,
who'll answer any question whether or
not she really knows.

197
00:15:36,300 --> 00:15:38,880
I'm not a regular mom — I'm a cool mom.

198
00:15:40,320 --> 00:15:44,320
That's AI trying to be helpful and
completely misreading the room.

199
00:15:45,380 --> 00:15:50,840
And your job, in all of this, is knowing
which parts to trust and which to push
back on.

200
00:15:51,660 --> 00:15:56,206
Which — good news — is exactly what
you've been doing with other people's

201
00:15:56,206 --> 00:16:01,980
work your entire career, and we're going
to help you apply those skills to AI
over the season.

202
00:16:03,280 --> 00:16:05,060
And... that's the episode.

203
00:16:05,060 --> 00:16:07,700
Now — your try-on.

204
00:16:08,560 --> 00:16:10,200
Not homework; this isn't school.

205
00:16:10,940 --> 00:16:14,900
Ten minutes, and everything you need
lives at laidies.ai.

206
00:16:15,560 --> 00:16:22,440
That's "LAiDIES" spelled with an i in
the middle: L, A, i, D, I, E, S.

207
00:16:23,400 --> 00:16:28,299
And if you'd rather read than listen,
the whole episode's written up there too

208
00:16:28,299 --> 00:16:32,020
— every stat and study laid out where
you can find it again.

209
00:16:33,460 --> 00:16:40,012
Inside is the try-on, and this week,
you're holding auditions: open the three

210
00:16:40,012 --> 00:16:47,320
big tools — ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini
— and give all three the same small,
real task.

211
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:51,380
My avoided email, or your version of it.

212
00:16:52,220 --> 00:16:56,866
Tell them the truth about who it's for
and what you need, then compare the

213
00:16:56,866 --> 00:16:58,600
three drafts that come back.

214
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:02,380
They won't match (that's the
editors-in-chief thing).

